hXUvjtdlb9F00kR","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Nicole Smedegaard","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2021-03-21T03:27:31.000Z","updated_at":"2023-03-10T03:27:31.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/aFbum3wBoEJfAmrgugWeI","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/aFbum3wBoEJfAmrgugWeI"}},{"id":"s3DIKvX5J48xeqmIKugeb","ext":"jpg","post_id":"XXD9JPkWnrfznDDC4bTVL","description":"One of the bigger rapids on the middle gorge. It is possible to scout from the river, but requires some light scrambling. Also somewhat serves as the entry into the middle gorge.  ","caption":"Middle gorge rapid","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Yann Crist-Evans","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2023-03-11T01:57:31.000Z","updated_at":"2023-03-14T01:57:31.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/s3DIKvX5J48xeqmIKugeb","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/s3DIKvX5J48xeqmIKugeb"}},{"id":"HgTvoK1rtxMTL2YiUAkuh","ext":"jpg","post_id":"dqP44TdCxjvI0mHBj0oA8","description":"This is the gauge that is reported on the flow phone, at the downstream end of the fish hatchery.  There is another gauge upstream that reads up to a foot higher.","caption":"Flow Phone Gauge","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Jacob Cruser","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-12-19T23:18:22.000Z","updated_at":"2025-01-06T23:18:22.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/HgTvoK1rtxMTL2YiUAkuh","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/HgTvoK1rtxMTL2YiUAkuh"}},{"id":"OewLNE9ZdtHBM2lXWQwhI","ext":"jpg","post_id":"XXD9JPkWnrfznDDC4bTVL","description":"Kelcy finds the sweet spot on the Boof, center, right of the first sequence.","caption":"First significant rapid on the upper section","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Yann Crist-Evans","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2023-03-11T01:57:31.000Z","updated_at":"2023-03-14T01:57:31.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/OewLNE9ZdtHBM2lXWQwhI","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/OewLNE9ZdtHBM2lXWQwhI"}},{"id":"gcN9HMUV9NdmBYWRQGufN","ext":"jpg","post_id":"XXD9JPkWnrfznDDC4bTVL","description":"Typical scenery on the upper section. ","caption":"Upper section ","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Yann Crist-Evans","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2023-03-11T01:57:31.000Z","updated_at":"2023-03-14T01:57:31.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/gcN9HMUV9NdmBYWRQGufN","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/gcN9HMUV9NdmBYWRQGufN"}},{"id":"jNfGhibBjfLL5fLQHK6iR","ext":"jpg","post_id":"dqP44TdCxjvI0mHBj0oA8","description":"This log needed to be portage by climbing over on the right at 3.7.  It is shortly below the landslide rapid on the upper section, on a right hand bend and can be seen from the shuttle road.","caption":"Log portage","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Jacob Cruser","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-12-19T23:18:22.000Z","updated_at":"2025-01-06T23:18:22.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/jNfGhibBjfLL5fLQHK6iR","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/jNfGhibBjfLL5fLQHK6iR"}},{"id":"aNefZ5kuAl94rDQN1chWX","ext":"jpg","post_id":"dqP44TdCxjvI0mHBj0oA8","description":"","caption":"Typical lower Elk Rapid","poi_name":null,"subject":"Priscilla Macy-Cruser","author":"Jacob Cruser","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-12-19T23:18:22.000Z","updated_at":"2025-01-06T23:18:22.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/aNefZ5kuAl94rDQN1chWX","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/aNefZ5kuAl94rDQN1chWX"}},{"id":"886804","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"889551","description":"A low, but enjoyable flow.  This gauge is located at the downstream end of the fish hatchery, a recording at (541) 332-0405 will often give a current reading of this gauge.","caption":"Gauge","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Jacob Cruser","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/886804","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/886804"}}],"detail":{"id":"1510","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"A great roadside run on the Wild and Scenic Elk River for the class IV paddler. Can be split into two runs with an upper run from Butler Creek to Purple Mountain and a lower run from Purple Mountain down to the Fish Hatchery.\n\nThe upper run is class II and III with one class IV. The lower run features about 5 class IV's, two harder than the rest. One of the big ones is visible from the road, but most of the others are not that easy to see. High water quality with good gradient and a tight gorge.  There is camping year round along the river, making this a good weekend option when other small streams around Oregon may be running a little low.\n\nThere is a good trip report linked below, with a neat video about the conservation of the area.  This is really one of Oregon's special places.\n\n[http://www.nwrafting.com/river-descriptions/the-elk-river](http://www.nwrafting.com/river-descriptions/the-elk-river)\n\nFor flows, call this number: (541) 332-0405.  3' is about the minimum recommended level as of 2016.  The right bank near the gauge is a cobble bar, so this gauge may vary slightly year to year.\n\n3.5-4' is probably the friendliest first-time range, while at higher levels the run is more consistently class IV.\n\nFull-size rafts will find the river navigable at flows at 5' and could go higher or a bit lower, while smaller R2s may find that 4' is still an enjoyable level for the lower section.\n\nNote that there are two gauges at the fish hatchery, the one that reports on the flow phone is downstream of the boat ramp/take out.  The one at the upstream end of the hatchery can read a full foot higher and is affected by an artificial dam.  **Use the lower gauge**.  Note that some of the comments on this page show pictures and refer to the upper gauge, so keep in mind that those reports refer to an inflated gauge reading, and the flow phone may have been reporting as much as a foot lower that day.\n\nLike the nearby Smith drainage, this river can be run at a wide range of flows without a large fluctuation in difficulty.","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"11.7","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["107867","113109"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-124.270998,42.726247],[-124.271274,42.726217],[-124.271296,42.726212],[-124.271371,42.726171],[-124.27217,42.725623],[-124.27219,42.725603],[-124.272241,42.725549],[-124.272285,42.72549],[-124.272311,42.725435],[-124.272325,42.725354],[-124.272328,42.725133],[-124.272305,42.725062],[-124.272266,42.724999],[-124.272153,42.724645],[-124.27212,42.724555],[-124.271968,42.724174],[-124.271949,42.72408],[-124.271951,42.723989],[-124.271938,42.7239],[-124.271824,42.723545],[-124.271791,42.723451],[-124.271639,42.723038],[-124.271591,42.722944],[-124.271358,42.722623],[-124.271314,42.722538],[-124.27125,42.722367],[-124.271235,42.722268],[-124.271239,42.722052],[-124.271265,42.721975],[-124.271311,42.721916],[-124.271475,42.721563],[-124.271519,42.721473],[-124.271885,42.720658],[-124.271942,42.720567],[-124.272077,42.720399],[-124.272161,42.720317],[-124.272249,42.720276],[-124.272324,42.720257],[-124.272432,42.720247],[-124.273205,42.720256],[-124.273245,42.720242],[-124.273345,42.720205],[-124.273454,42.720154],[-124.273555,42.720104],[-124.273605,42.720058],[-124.273631,42.720004],[-124.273632,42.719923],[-124.273487,42.719677],[-124.27342,42.719606],[-124.273163,42.719442],[-124.273108,42.719384],[-124.273084,42.71933],[-124.273073,42.719267],[-124.273093,42.719177],[-124.273209,42.718878],[-124.273254,42.718783],[-124.273357,42.718616],[-124.273424,42.718529],[-124.273592,42.718361],[-124.273